WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.810
Tamar: Well, you know, in the '50s the working
class, the industrial working class were the

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.720
heroes in America and they haven't been the
heroes in America for a long, long time and

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.750
so they're hurting in a lot of ways.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:22.300
Robert: Tamar Jacoby, President of Opportunity
America.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.300
Thanks for being here.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.300
Tamar: My pleasure, Robert.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.369
Robert: And we're here to talk about a report
that you were the driving force behind "Work,

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:29.670
Skills, and Community".

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.180
Tamar: And you were a part of, too, and we
had a lot of fun doing it.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.280
Robert: We did.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.360
Why did we do it and what is it about?

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.460
Tamar: It's about the working class.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.579
And I'm sure everyone remembers the campaign
for...in 2016 the working class was all anyone

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:46.360
could talk about, and there were furious sound
bites on every side of the campaign, a lot

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.100
of journalism but I don't think anyone really
got to the bottom of who these people are

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.520
and what their issues are.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.270
And when we as a group of scholars started
to look beneath all of this sound and fury

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:02.530
and sound bites we found that they're a group
still somewhere between the poorest of the

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.539
poor and the middle class but having troubles.

00:01:05.539 --> 00:01:10.060
They used to look like the middle class in
all but the income range and now they're starting

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.560
to look like the poorest of the poor in many
ways.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.799
And so there's some real issues out there
that have been festering for decades that

00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:20.219
no one's really paid attention to that the
2016 campaign, for better or worse, at least

00:01:20.219 --> 00:01:23.650
ripped the cover off and got us to look, but
we wanted to look deeper and see if we could

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.849
come up with some solutions and we wanted
to do it in a bipartisan way.

00:01:26.849 --> 00:01:29.979
Robert: And we found that their anger was
justified in some regard.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:32.829
The economy's not been terrific for them.

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:34.439
What do we think are some good answers?

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:38.780
Tamar: Well, there's...we came up with a range
of answers and our...the theme...so we have

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:45.039
three dozen answers in our book and the common
denominator is work.

00:01:45.039 --> 00:01:49.310
We wanna make it...we wanna make...get more
people back into the workforce.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.639
We wanna make work pay.

00:01:51.639 --> 00:01:56.250
We wanna give people the education and skills
to get work and better work and better jobs

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.950
and we wanna strengthen families and communities
to support work.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:06.849
So the solutions range from unions to childcare
to college financing but the themes, the big

00:02:06.849 --> 00:02:11.060
ideas are all around work and making work...putting
the work back in working class.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.430
Robert: So let's talk about skills.

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:15.310
Bachelor's degree, a good idea for everybody?

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:19.909
Tamar: No, and the nation has been trying
to sell college successfully,

00:02:19.909 --> 00:02:24.489
selling college for all for two or three decades
and what that has meant is a lot of kids have

00:02:24.489 --> 00:02:28.239
gone to college who weren't really gonna do
that well in college and it's not because

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:29.519
they're not as smart.

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:32.670
It's just because they may be different or
the jobs they want don't need a four year

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:33.900
college.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.290
So people are now starting to realize across
the country that maybe that was the wrong

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.290
focus.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.409
I mean, if you can get to college and get
through and succeed and have the right major,

00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:45.030
four year college is a great investment.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:49.189
But if you don't get through or you pick the
wrong major, a very important choice, or you

00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:53.140
go into debt you're...it's...often you're
worse off at the end.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.000
So we gave a lot of thought to what could
a true alternative to college be for this

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.069
group, those in this group who for that, whom
that makes sense.

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:02.499
And how are we gonna pay for it.

00:03:02.499 --> 00:03:06.799
Because right now the bulk of federal higher
ed financing goes to traditional academic

00:03:06.799 --> 00:03:12.659
college and very little of it, just 14% of
it goes to what I call career education or

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:14.049
occupational education.

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:17.620
And, you know, I don't know exactly what the
balance should be, occupational or academic

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.269
but it's certainly not 14-86.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:25.670
And so we gave a lot of thought to how to...to
what...how we can do a policy should be during

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:30.200
a time of national experimentation which is
a tricky question because the feds can't...shouldn't

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.019
just be deciding to throw a billion dollars
at this.

00:03:33.019 --> 00:03:37.099
They...we have to be encouraging experimentation
and creating standards but also...

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:41.019
And also how to get employers involved, that's
another big question, but then that final,

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:45.230
you know, harder to wrestle question is where
to find the money.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.379
Robert: So this was one of those right and
left coming together.

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:53.349
So we had six scholars from the left and six
scholars and experts from the right.

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:57.969
What did the right agree to that they might
not normally think they would agree to and

00:03:57.969 --> 00:04:01.010
what did the left agree to that you don't
think they would've normally agreed to?

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:07.629
Tamar: Yeah, so the left agreed to modest
work requirements for some government programs.

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:12.489
I mean, everyone in the group more or less
agreed that the social...that the agencies

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:18.410
that administer these programs don't do enough
to get people into the workforce, that mostly

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:22.930
they give out the benefits and they don't
do enough to get people into jobs and everybody

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:28.071
was okay with that, with giving the...a new
mandate to the agencies to try harder to get

00:04:28.071 --> 00:04:29.071
people into jobs.

00:04:29.071 --> 00:04:31.300
Robert: And the programs are like SNAP, food
stamps, Medicaid.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.819
Tamar: SNAP, the food stamps, Medicaid, everybody,
yes, yes, thank you.

00:04:34.819 --> 00:04:39.820
But where we differed and where a few people
on the left gave little ground was whether

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:45.349
we should actually require people who get
benefits to work or to do some kind of work

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:46.349
activity.

00:04:46.349 --> 00:04:51.510
And, you know, 87% of the public I think the
number is thinks that that should be but there

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.599
are some people in the left who think that
if you're talking about Medicaid perhaps or

00:04:54.599 --> 00:04:56.500
food stamps you shouldn't have those requirements.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.569
So that was a little movement on the left.

00:04:59.569 --> 00:05:05.830
On the right we were willing to get...find
a few little tax increases.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:11.509
We focused on tax increases that were places
where people who benefited from the corporate

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:17.139
tax cut, you know, in the upper end of the
income scale maybe have benefited, I won't

00:05:17.139 --> 00:05:20.860
say disproportionately, but a lot, and we
have some modest taxes there.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:25.630
But a lot of things...and this is...a lot
of our proposals we found ways to pay for

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:29.910
by, you know, as we used to call it cutting
waste and fraud and misguided spending.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:34.629
I mean, on the education front that we were
just talking about, education skills, we found

00:05:34.629 --> 00:05:42.430
in double digit billions by eliminating 529
education savings account, for example, which

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.900
go overwhelmingly to the upper middle class.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.879
You know, our thinking about the...the people
that we're thinking about is they're kind

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:49.250
of the hole in the donut.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:53.790
They're a little too well off to benefit from
many programs that are for the poor but they

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:58.080
don't earn enough or pay taxes or pay tuition
so they don't benefit from any of the programs

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.420
for the middle class.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.659
And that ranges, you know...I mean, it's true
to some degree the mortgage interest deduction

00:06:02.659 --> 00:06:07.210
is less, but certainly education kinda benefits
and childcare benefits.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:11.080
And so we wanna even some of that out and
so we've...in a lot of cases we found the

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:15.069
money that we were gonna spend on the working
class in other places.

00:06:15.069 --> 00:06:16.480
Robert: Subsidies for the well off.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:17.480
Tamar: For the well off.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:23.699
Robert: So skills development, a little bit
of work requirements in federal programs that

00:06:23.699 --> 00:06:27.720
provide assistance without focusing on employment.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.440
What are some of the easy solutions that some
politicians talk about that we rejected?

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.539
Tamar: Good, a very good question because
there are a lot of solutions floating around

00:06:35.539 --> 00:06:41.110
out there that sound great and they fit on
your bumper sticker and universal basic income,

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:43.949
free college, Medicaid for everybody.

00:06:43.949 --> 00:06:44.949
We said no.

00:06:44.949 --> 00:06:45.949
We don't like those.

00:06:45.949 --> 00:06:47.379
Most of them would be harmful.

00:06:47.379 --> 00:06:49.310
Our solutions are more practical.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:51.930
Often they've been...I mean, you know, so
let's take an example.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.129
Universal basic income.

00:06:53.129 --> 00:06:56.280
We think that it should be universal basic
work, universal work.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.440
We should help everybody get a job, not give
everybody a subsidy to stay home and this...and

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:04.039
productive, people like us will work and a
lot of other people won't work and won't have

00:07:04.039 --> 00:07:05.430
the dignity of work.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:11.330
And so we rejected these bumper sticker solutions
and our solutions, you know, they can sound

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:12.330
wonky.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:16.870
I'll be the first to admit they don't have...most
of them don't fit on a bumper sticker and

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:20.240
they need to be argued through but, you know,
we think they're practical.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.780
We think they now have at least bipartisan
buy in of some kind.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.750
Many of them have been tested in states or
are being tested in states and many of them

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:33.620
have been floating around on Capitol Hill
and the one...with some bipartisan support

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:37.060
but the one reason we couldn't...we haven't
been able to enact them is because people

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:38.849
couldn't agree where to find the money.

00:07:38.849 --> 00:07:44.530
And so for example on the expanding EITC for
childless workers Obama has talked about that

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.310
and Paul Ryan has talked about that.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:48.480
What they couldn't agree on was where to find
the money.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.930
We came up with a bipartisan solution for
where to find the money.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.539
Robert: And one more on what we didn't choose.

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:57.849
We didn't choose protectionism or a global
trade war.

00:07:57.849 --> 00:08:00.490
Tamar: No, or a wall at the border or...

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:01.490
Robert: Ending immigration.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:05.219
Tamar: Or ending immigration and, you know,
we understand that there's a lot of people

00:08:05.219 --> 00:08:09.099
in the working class who feel that...who feel
or have been told that immigration is the

00:08:09.099 --> 00:08:13.650
source of their problem or have latched on
to immigration as the source of their problem.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:14.889
We don't think it is.

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:18.659
Certainly not the major source and maybe not
even a significant source and so we felt we

00:08:18.659 --> 00:08:24.770
could come up with our solutions that we think
really are real solutions without going near

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:26.050
that very contentious issue.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:31.130
Robert: So just describe for me...to be clear
who are we talking about?

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.290
What is the category or...

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:33.290
Tamar: Yeah, exactly.

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:34.290
[inaudible 00:08:32] working class.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:35.290
Who are they?

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:39.291
So they have at least a high school diploma
but not a four year college degree and they

00:08:39.291 --> 00:08:46.570
live in a household...the technical description
is between the 20th and 50th income percentile.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:51.330
What that means in practice for a household
that has 2 parents and a child I believe is

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.900
between $30K and $70K a year.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:58.330
Robert: And the perception in the past was
that someone with a high school degree but

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:03.690
not a college who wanted to work and participate
in the economy could do well enough to support

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:06.310
a family and that's not true right now.

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:12.270
Tamar: The globalization and technology and
the way the country has shifted, everything

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:15.720
from...everything in policy, I dare say, where
we've just talked about a minute ago, middle

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.750
class and poor getting benefits, upper middle
class, not these people, but also where investment

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:21.750
goes.

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:25.520
Seventy five percent of the venture capital
investment goes to three states.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:29.310
So the...a lot of people in a lot of places
have just been left behind and they've been

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:34.030
left behind materially, economically, what
have you, but they've also been left behind

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:35.030
politically.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:40.300
Nobody seriously thought about them and tried
to have answers for them or took them seriously

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:41.720
or, you know, gave them the dignity.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:47.090
I mean, I'm not this old but, you know, in
the '50s the working class, and the industrial

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:51.080
working class were the heroes in America and
they haven't been the heroes in America for

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:54.090
a long, long time and so they're hurting in
a lot of ways.

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:59.460
Robert: So "Work, Skills, Community," we've
talked about work, we've talked about skills.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:02.360
We also ask something of this group as well,
don't we?

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:07.120
Tamar: We do and community is the hardest
one, right, because what...how much can government

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.140
reach the community and how much can, you
know, the...any level of government, what

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:15.820
can they do to create new community and how
much can government really do to repair marriages

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:17.180
and families?

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.180
And those things...

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:22.610
Marriage, I mean, some of our really troubling
factual findings, marriage has declined more

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:25.900
in our group than in any other group over
the past 20 years.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:29.920
It was already a bit low for the poor but
it's declined there but it's...and it's declined...the

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.150
middle class are doing pretty well.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:34.820
Working class, down, down, down.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:40.770
And the same way with single parent families
and similarly with social, you know, interaction

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:42.340
with people in your community.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:46.000
So this is...these are...they're not just
hurting in that they've had to go from a $25

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.260
an hour factory job to a $9 an hour Wendy's
job, they're hurting because their communities

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:52.580
are falling apart and the bowling league…

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:54.900
Robert: Things that held them together are
frayed.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:58.050
Tamar: The things that held them together,
the churches and the unions and the...and

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:01.330
all of those things are frayed or gone.

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:03.030
And so it's...

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:10.370
And the opioid crisis has preyed on, you know,
and filled in the crumbling, you know, rubble.

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:13.450
So, you know, what can government do about
that?

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:18.140
We encourage people to follow the success
sequence to get a certain amount of education

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:20.940
and get a job and get married before you have
children.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:24.700
You know, we can't make anybody do that and
the government can't...you know, we can't

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:25.761
punish people who don't but we...

00:11:25.761 --> 00:11:27.310
Robert: We also call on employers to do more,
don't we?

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:29.180
Tamar: We call on employers to do more in
that realm.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:36.080
We call on employers to do more in a lot of
realms, both that realm, childcare, also in

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:37.400
getting involved in career education.

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:41.280
You can't really teach...prepare somebody
for a job without knowing what job you're

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:43.910
preparing them for and talking to the employer
about it.

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:44.910
So we go...

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:46.850
Robert: Often the best training takes place
inside employers.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:47.890
Tamar: Absolutely.

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:51.520
Or with employers cooperating with the training
provider of some kind.

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:56.650
But so...and we also...so we struggled with
how to strengthen the communities.

00:11:56.650 --> 00:12:02.560
Employers, working class men and women themselves,
families, we're kind of excited about the

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:09.800
potential of opportunity zones, new provision
in the 2017 Tax Act that will presumably send

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.770
some more money out into these places but
will only, investment capital, but will only

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:20.140
work, we think, if these places get their
act together to solve some of the other problems

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:21.140
around the...

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:26.510
I mean, my example is, you know, that City
X is gonna become the 3D innovation hub in

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:27.510
America.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:30.960
Well, that would be great if they could get
some investment to build that but it won't

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:36.070
work if they're not training people to be
3D innovation technicians.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:42.260
Robert: So you led us, our group, the six
from the left and the six from the right,

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:47.480
on some traveling out into the country and
we went to West Michigan and that was a pretty

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:48.540
good role model.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:49.540
What did you see?

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:52.100
Tamar: We saw amazing civic leadership in
West Michigan.

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:56.290
We went to Grand Rapids and what has happened
in Grand Rapids over the last 20 years is

00:12:56.290 --> 00:13:01.060
that basically the business leaders with the
government, you know, in cahoots and social

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:05.170
services as well has come together to say,
"We're gonna make this city work and we're

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:06.650
not gonna wait for the federal government.

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:07.980
We're not gonna go begging Congress.

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:10.920
We're gonna put our money where we need to,
and mostly I think important, we're gonna

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:15.190
coordinate and plan together and come up with
a plan that we then all follow."

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:20.880
And, you know, we saw a city that was just
remarkable in how...what...how well things

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:21.930
seem to be working.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.390
And we didn't...you know, we were there two
days so I'm sure there's some potholes...

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:27.340
Robert: Yeah, it was clear to us that that
is part of the solution as well.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:28.340
Tamar: Absolutely.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:31.910
And that's part of what we hope...our thinking
about opportunity zones will generate because

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:36.320
we're seeing some of that in some other places,
come together and make a plan, execute it

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:39.220
together and that, you know, and attract some
money while you're doing it.

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:40.850
Robert: It was remarkable.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:47.190
Community, employers, educational institutions
working together to help people get the skills

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:48.880
they need to have higher wages.

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:49.880
That's the key, right?

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:52.801
Tamar: I think that's the key and I think
what might happen...this hard part that we're

00:13:52.801 --> 00:13:56.570
trying to address which is families coming
back together and communities forming could

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:57.920
happen in that environment.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.150
Robert: Thank you.

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:00.390
Tamar: My pleasure.

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:01.630
Robert: Hey, everyone.

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:04.220
That's the end of our discussion with Tamar
Jacoby.

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:08.581
Thanks for watching and as always let us know
